    topicTag: "patient-education",
    payload: {"setting":"Hospital ward, at the bedside, with discharge planned for this afternoon.","candidateRole":"You are the ward nurse. The patient is medically fit for discharge after treatment for community-acquired pneumonia.","patientRole":"A 68-year-old who lives alone in a first-floor flat, has been breathless at night on the ward, and has twice asked whether she could stay one more day.","patientConcern":"The patient is afraid the breathlessness will return during the night and that nobody will be there to help.","prepSeconds":180,"speakSeconds":300,"candidateCard":"Ask what specifically worries her about going home, and let her name it rather than guessing.\n\nAsk how the nights have been on the ward and what she does when the breathlessness comes.\n\nAcknowledge the fear and treat it as a practical problem to be solved rather than as reluctance.\n\nExplain what has improved: the observations, the oxygen levels and the chest, so the discharge has visible reasons.\n\nExplain the support arranged: the community nurse visit, the follow-up appointment and who checks on her.\n\nGo through the medication with her, including the antibiotic course, what each is for and when to take it.\n\nExplain exactly what to do if the breathlessness returns at night, in steps, including who to call at which hour.\n\nAsk practical questions about home: stairs, shopping, heating, and who has a key.\n\nOffer what can still be arranged today, including a personal alarm and a call from the ward tomorrow.\n\nAgree the plan, write it down, and check she can say back what she would do at three in the morning."},
