    topicTag: "advice",
    payload: {"setting":"Diabetes clinic in a community health centre, a follow-up after the doctor recommended starting insulin.","candidateRole":"You are the practice nurse. The patient's type 2 diabetes is no longer controlled on maximum oral therapy and insulin has been recommended.","patientRole":"A 56-year-old delivery driver who has had type 2 diabetes for nine years, has always managed on tablets and diet, and has an HbA1c of 84 mmol/mol.","patientConcern":"The patient believes needing insulin means they have failed and that the diabetes is now severe, and fears self-injecting and losing the driving licence and the job.","prepSeconds":180,"speakSeconds":300,"candidateCard":"Ask how they feel about what the doctor said last week, and give room for more than a practical answer.\n\nAsk what insulin means to them, and where that idea has come from.\n\nCorrect the sense of failure directly: explain that the pancreas changes over years regardless of what they do.\n\nExplain why insulin is being suggested now and what the current numbers mean for their eyes, kidneys and feet.\n\nExplain what starting it would actually involve: the pen, the needle size, how often, and how it is done.\n\nShow the device and let them handle it, and offer to inject saline so it stops being imaginary.\n\nAddress driving honestly: what must be reported, what the glucose testing rules are, and how other drivers manage it.\n\nExplain hypoglycaemia: what it feels like, what to carry, and what to do, so that the fear has a procedure attached.\n\nAsk what would make the first week easier, including telephone support and who else needs to know.\n\nAgree a practical next step, even if that is a further appointment rather than starting today."},
